WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.850 --> 00:00:03.950
When I began my career in cancer research,

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:05.050
really,

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:09.800
nothing was known about how the disease arises,

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:13.660
and now we know exactly why cancer occurs.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:15.670
We don't know all the causes,

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:16.650
which is a problem,

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:22.150
but we do know that once those causes have done their job,

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:26.300
all cancer arises from the malfunction of genes.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:35.530
When I arrived to take up my position at University of 

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:36.010
California,

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:36.880
San Francisco,

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:38.780
I met Warren Levinson,

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:43.860
who was another newly recruited young scientist who had been 

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:48.450
trained to study Rous sarcoma virus.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:53.780
Now Rous sarcoma virus was discovered by Peyton 

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:55.950
Rous at the turn of the 20th century,

00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:00.550
and was the first tumor virus ever described 

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:02.790
to any credible level.

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.250
It was a historic discovery,

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:11.270
and it was utterly neglected for over fifty years. This was a chicken virus;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:16.070
this virus was discovered in chickens originally. And did it bother me that it was a chicken virus?

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:16.700
Not at all.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:19.820
I was a great believer in what we call the universality of nature,

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.150
and if I could find out anything about cancer in a chicken,

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:25.950
I knew it would apply to humans.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:27.310
And boy,

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:27.950
was I right.

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:30.640
The biological effects were dramatic.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:35.450
You could take normal chicken fiberglass in a Petri dish,

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:39.210
put Rous sarcoma virus on it, on those cells,

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:40.750
and within 24 hours,

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:44.420
their whole phenotype changed,

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:49.640
their appearance changed, their replicative behavior changed to be more aggressive.

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:55.000
They started crawling over each other in a chaotic 

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.120
manner, like cancer cells can do.

00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:57.300
And,

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:57.700
of course,

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:59.340
if you put the virus into a chicken,

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:00.950
it would also get a tumor.

00:02:02.075 --> 00:02:04.025
That's what Rous had originally done,

00:02:04.035 --> 00:02:05.655
that's how he discovered the virus.

00:02:07.345 --> 00:02:11.195
He took a tumor, lysed the cells,

00:02:11.375 --> 00:02:14.595
put it through a filter to take out any living cells,

00:02:15.665 --> 00:02:18.655
put that lysate, or filtrate as we called it,

00:02:21.315 --> 00:02:25.195
into chickens, and they would get the same tumor.

00:02:25.395 --> 00:02:28.955
And then he could make extracts from that tumor,

00:02:29.865 --> 00:02:33.095
filter it, put it in another chicken, and get a tumor again.

00:02:33.745 --> 00:02:34.185
Hence,

00:02:34.185 --> 00:02:38.855
Rous sarcoma virus. Another very important discovery that was made was 

00:02:38.855 --> 00:02:43.785
overshadowed to an unfortunate extent by the discovery of reverse 

00:02:43.785 --> 00:02:44.715
transcriptase.

00:02:44.725 --> 00:02:49.065
And that was a discovery, made by Steven Martin at the University of California,

00:02:49.065 --> 00:02:49.555
Berkeley,

00:02:51.745 --> 00:02:55.505
that Rous sarcoma virus had at least one gene,

00:02:55.505 --> 00:03:00.385
and it turned out to be only one gene, that was responsible for the tumorigenic 

00:03:00.695 --> 00:03:02.425
capacity of the virus.

00:03:02.945 --> 00:03:04.865
And that brings me to Harold Varmus,

00:03:05.445 --> 00:03:10.075
who joined me as a colleague in 1970, the year that reverse 

00:03:10.075 --> 00:03:14.855
transcriptase was discovered and two years after I started my work at 

00:03:14.865 --> 00:03:18.975
UCSF. Harold joined me in an 

00:03:19.285 --> 00:03:22.055
extremely informal process.

00:03:23.215 --> 00:03:28.215
He had been a postdoctoral fellow at the National Institutes 

00:03:28.215 --> 00:03:32.785
of Health and had taken a 

00:03:32.785 --> 00:03:35.295
course in tumor viruses.

00:03:35.995 --> 00:03:38.905
He was working at the time with phage and bacteria.

00:03:38.975 --> 00:03:41.425
And I think about five minutes into the conversation,

00:03:41.425 --> 00:03:46.055
I decided we ought to get this guy in the lab because he was obviously very smart 

00:03:46.745 --> 00:03:49.115
and we were on the same wavelength scientifically;

00:03:49.115 --> 00:03:53.865
we wanted to do molecular biology on the virus and we shared other 

00:03:53.865 --> 00:03:54.295
interests,

00:03:54.295 --> 00:03:55.395
cultural interests,

00:03:56.445 --> 00:03:58.045
particularly literary interests.

00:03:58.945 --> 00:04:03.194
We decided that we ought to look for src in normal cells.

00:04:03.224 --> 00:04:03.344
Now,

00:04:03.344 --> 00:04:05.404
this was well before recombinant DNA.

00:04:05.404 --> 00:04:06.734
If we'd had recombinant DNA,

00:04:06.734 --> 00:04:09.744
we could have done the subsequent experiments in a few months.

00:04:10.134 --> 00:04:10.564
As it is,

00:04:10.564 --> 00:04:11.674
it took four years.

00:04:12.914 --> 00:04:15.424
And here we were helped again by genetics.

00:04:16.164 --> 00:04:16.944
Peter Vogt,

00:04:17.544 --> 00:04:20.374
a longstanding collaborator of ours,

00:04:21.044 --> 00:04:25.554
Peter Vogt was a master of the biology of these viruses and the genetics.

00:04:26.424 --> 00:04:31.104
And he had isolated mutants in Rous sarcoma virus 

00:04:31.864 --> 00:04:33.814
that were deletions of src.

00:04:35.504 --> 00:04:40.174
So we had available to us the deletion mutant, which had 

00:04:41.414 --> 00:04:44.074
no src or a very little piece of src in it,

00:04:45.924 --> 00:04:46.514
and of course,

00:04:46.514 --> 00:04:49.574
we had the full Rous sarcoma virus genome with src in it.

00:04:50.654 --> 00:04:55.354
So the idea was that we would copy, use reverse transcriptase to 

00:04:55.354 --> 00:04:59.074
copy the genome of Rous sarcoma virus into radioactive DNA,

00:04:59.754 --> 00:05:04.274
and then we would use the deletion mutant to fish, to pull out,

00:05:05.454 --> 00:05:06.874
by molecular hybridization,

00:05:07.764 --> 00:05:12.634
we would use the deletion mutant to pull out all of the nucleotide 

00:05:12.634 --> 00:05:17.484
sequences of the replicated genes, and leave 

00:05:17.494 --> 00:05:21.584
the src nucleotides in the residue,

00:05:22.064 --> 00:05:22.614
if you will.

00:05:23.974 --> 00:05:25.074
It worked like a charm.

00:05:26.394 --> 00:05:27.874
It was hard work.

00:05:28.364 --> 00:05:33.304
The first hints that it would work came from a postdoctoral 

00:05:33.594 --> 00:05:34.884
fellow named Ramareddy Guntaka,

00:05:36.164 --> 00:05:38.824
but he had a full blown project well underway,

00:05:38.824 --> 00:05:42.304
and once he had shown that we could probably make a probe this way,

00:05:42.634 --> 00:05:45.974
we recruited another postdoctoral fellow, Dominique Stehelin,

00:05:47.764 --> 00:05:50.624
to run with it and he did, in spades.

00:05:52.464 --> 00:05:57.214
We began to make this src specific probe, we were able to demonstrate 

00:05:57.554 --> 00:05:59.634
its specificity in a variety of ways.

00:06:00.854 --> 00:06:04.457
He was able to make this probe consistently,

00:06:05.707 --> 00:06:10.557
and to use it, and soon found 

00:06:10.947 --> 00:06:13.777
that the probe would react with normal chicken DNA.

00:06:13.977 --> 00:06:17.047
If you want to use the probe 

00:06:18.947 --> 00:06:23.357
to assess the presence of the sequences in the probe in DNA,

00:06:23.947 --> 00:06:25.857
you first have to take the DNA apart,

00:06:27.107 --> 00:06:28.767
denature it as they say,

00:06:28.777 --> 00:06:29.857
the strands separate,

00:06:30.447 --> 00:06:34.447
and then you put the radioactive probe in there and you allow the 

00:06:35.127 --> 00:06:39.997
strands to reassociate. And the radioactive probe gets 

00:06:40.007 --> 00:06:41.387
taken up in the mix.

00:06:43.247 --> 00:06:48.057
So although the bulk of the DNA will reassociate with its unlabeled 

00:06:48.447 --> 00:06:49.547
original compliment,

00:06:49.887 --> 00:06:52.637
some of it will re-anneal with the radioactive probe.

00:06:53.047 --> 00:06:57.657
There was something in the DNA of normal avian cells that was homologous 

00:06:58.307 --> 00:07:01.337
to the oncogene, the cancer gene, of Rous sarcoma virus.

00:07:02.417 --> 00:07:04.697
We called it cell src for the sake of the argument.

00:07:04.967 --> 00:07:06.987
If you fiddled with the reaction conditions,

00:07:07.507 --> 00:07:11.057
the probe would react with DNA from any avian species we tested,

00:07:11.537 --> 00:07:14.047
including the most primitive species,

00:07:14.047 --> 00:07:18.027
such as ostrich or the so called ratite, the ostrich,

00:07:18.027 --> 00:07:23.007
the emu, the rhea. Incidentally, it was not easy to come by the DNA for 

00:07:23.007 --> 00:07:23.647
these creatures,

00:07:23.647 --> 00:07:25.867
but we managed one way or another.

00:07:26.927 --> 00:07:31.757
I vividly remember the morning that Dominique brought 

00:07:31.757 --> 00:07:33.657
us the first positive results.

00:07:34.247 --> 00:07:39.117
They'd come in a late Saturday evening when Harold and I, 

00:07:40.247 --> 00:07:43.557
who were in a partnership at the time that lasted for fifteen years,

00:07:44.207 --> 00:07:45.457
first saw the data.

00:07:49.497 --> 00:07:50.477
I was dumbfounded.

00:07:52.947 --> 00:07:55.257
I recognize that this was heretical 

00:07:57.347 --> 00:08:01.827
discovery and that we were going to have to construct an 

00:08:01.827 --> 00:08:02.957
airtight argument.

00:08:03.707 --> 00:08:08.517
So I think I was a little less enthusiastic than Dominique would have liked because he was beside 

00:08:08.517 --> 00:08:10.037
himself with excitement.

00:08:11.827 --> 00:08:16.637
But Harold and I both understood from the get go that if this was real,

00:08:18.427 --> 00:08:22.277
we were looking, we were looking at a possible 

00:08:22.917 --> 00:08:27.657
entrée to the very fundamental underpinning of cancer.

00:08:33.927 --> 00:08:37.477
By the time I gave my Nobel lecture,

00:08:38.227 --> 00:08:43.087
I had a slide with something like thirty retroviral 

00:08:43.427 --> 00:08:43.967
oncogenes,

00:08:43.977 --> 00:08:47.637
each of which had a demonstrable proto-oncogene 

00:08:49.027 --> 00:08:49.687
progenitor,

00:08:49.687 --> 00:08:50.237
if you will,

00:08:50.837 --> 00:08:53.997
in normal cells. And as the number mounted,

00:08:53.997 --> 00:08:58.927
it became clear that here was, here was the makings of 

00:08:58.927 --> 00:09:02.687
a unifying theory of cancer. 

00:09:04.157 --> 00:09:08.877
And we were cautious about how we 

00:09:09.187 --> 00:09:11.337
stated that publicly at first,

00:09:11.927 --> 00:09:13.577
but by the early 80s,

00:09:14.427 --> 00:09:19.137
I was using that term, unifying theory, of how 

00:09:19.627 --> 00:09:21.347
all cancers come about.

00:09:21.407 --> 00:09:26.077
It was a gradually building thrill to 

00:09:26.077 --> 00:09:30.437
realize that we had the cancer cell by the neck, in terms of 

00:09:30.437 --> 00:09:31.337
understanding it,

00:09:32.327 --> 00:09:34.447
not in terms of throttling it,

00:09:34.617 --> 00:09:36.537
but in terms of understanding it.

